WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.600
&gt;&gt; Okay, good morning.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.286
It's my pleasure to be
here. Thanks for having me.

00:00:08.286 --> 00:00:09.810
And so today I'll talk about

00:00:09.811 --> 00:00:11.730
a topic that I'm actually
very excited about,

00:00:11.731 --> 00:00:14.025
which is dealing with
rare data in computer vision.

00:00:14.026 --> 00:00:17.370
I've worked on
this starting from my PhD,

00:00:17.371 --> 00:00:20.980
and I'm still continuing
with similar topic.

00:00:20.981 --> 00:00:24.175
So why is it that I
like this problem?

00:00:24.176 --> 00:00:25.470
Well, it turns out that actually

00:00:25.471 --> 00:00:27.370
the world behaves like that.

00:00:27.371 --> 00:00:29.585
For some reason it gives us

00:00:29.586 --> 00:00:32.775
more data for some concepts
are very popular.

00:00:32.776 --> 00:00:36.810
But for other concepts
we have only a few data,

00:00:36.811 --> 00:00:38.700
or it is very hard to
collect data, right?

00:00:38.701 --> 00:00:42.550
So for example, you can see
this on the 397 data set, so.

00:00:42.551 --> 00:00:44.190
And then pull up
the histogram of

00:00:44.191 --> 00:00:46.265
the instances that you
have in that data set,

00:00:46.266 --> 00:00:48.270
you will see that
the living room, actually,

00:00:48.271 --> 00:00:49.620
is the concept that has

00:00:49.621 --> 00:00:52.015
the most instances to
actually work with.

00:00:52.016 --> 00:00:56.445
But when you actually
find a skateboard park,

00:00:56.446 --> 00:01:02.315
you only have a few
instances to work with.

00:01:02.316 --> 00:01:05.550
To give you another instance
of this problem,

00:01:05.551 --> 00:01:09.510
feature matching is
another good example of this.

00:01:09.511 --> 00:01:12.530
So when you actually match
features from two images,

00:01:12.531 --> 00:01:14.250
many of the candidate
correspondences

00:01:14.251 --> 00:01:15.975
that you get are actually wrong.

00:01:15.976 --> 00:01:19.120
So only a few portion of
them are actually good, okay?

00:01:19.121 --> 00:01:20.870
So in nature,

00:01:20.871 --> 00:01:23.060
these two correspondences
are actually rare.

00:01:23.061 --> 00:01:26.245
Okay? And, well,

00:01:26.246 --> 00:01:27.800
we actually have to learn how to

00:01:27.801 --> 00:01:29.455
deal with these rare events.

00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:31.690
For example, consider
a self-driving car, right?

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:34.100
Many of those systems,

00:01:34.101 --> 00:01:36.245
for the perception systems
that they have,

00:01:36.246 --> 00:01:37.660
very likely they have,

00:01:37.661 --> 00:01:41.310
or they're trained
with, the common scenes

00:01:41.311 --> 00:01:44.015
or common events of
the world, right?

00:01:44.016 --> 00:01:46.520
But consider the case,
I don't know,

00:01:46.521 --> 00:01:48.715
that this self-driving
car is actually

00:01:48.716 --> 00:01:51.815
seeing this baby
crawling on the street.

00:01:51.816 --> 00:01:54.620
It is a very unlikely event,
but actually do happen.

00:01:54.621 --> 00:01:56.140
Actually when I was
creating this slide,

00:01:56.141 --> 00:01:59.515
I found a few posts

00:01:59.516 --> 00:02:01.785
in the news where
actually this happen.

00:02:01.786 --> 00:02:05.935
So if we don't learn how to
deal with these rare events,

00:02:05.936 --> 00:02:08.455
we can have horrible
outcomes, right?

00:02:08.456 --> 00:02:11.450
So, I organized
the talk as follows.

00:02:11.451 --> 00:02:12.890
So first, I'm going to talk

00:02:12.891 --> 00:02:14.495
about what I did,
essentially in my PhD,

00:02:14.496 --> 00:02:16.625
dealing with correspondences,

00:02:16.626 --> 00:02:18.875
and treat them as rare events.

00:02:18.876 --> 00:02:21.185
And then I will switch to

00:02:21.186 --> 00:02:23.755
current topics that I'm
actually working on right now.

00:02:23.756 --> 00:02:25.070
So the second one is learning

00:02:25.071 --> 00:02:27.400
directly from
a long-tail data set.

00:02:27.401 --> 00:02:29.850
And then the third one is mainly

00:02:29.851 --> 00:02:32.850
just a visualization
framework for CNN's,

00:02:32.851 --> 00:02:34.880
dealing with pixel level tasks.

00:02:34.881 --> 00:02:37.360
But it has a relationship
with rare events.

00:02:37.361 --> 00:02:40.230
Especially, I was
interested in seeing how

00:02:40.231 --> 00:02:42.340
CNN's behave for input

00:02:42.341 --> 00:02:44.635
that the CNN has
never seen before.

00:02:44.636 --> 00:02:47.210
And then I'll just
conclude, okay?

00:02:47.211 --> 00:02:49.155
So I'm going to start

00:02:49.156 --> 00:02:53.490
with correspondences,
and the feature matching.

00:02:53.491 --> 00:02:57.610
And this work essentially aims

00:02:57.611 --> 00:03:02.875
to improve RANSAC-based
algorithms,

00:03:02.876 --> 00:03:06.615
to make them faster,
but with a noble insight

00:03:06.616 --> 00:03:10.940
of treating these two
correspondences as rare events.

00:03:10.941 --> 00:03:12.660
And I'm going to
be using statistics

00:03:12.661 --> 00:03:14.725
in order to model
those rare events,

00:03:14.726 --> 00:03:16.070
in order to do so.

00:03:16.071 --> 00:03:19.240
This was joint work with
other collaborators,

00:03:19.241 --> 00:03:20.610
Sergio Rodriguez, Pradeep Sen,

00:03:20.611 --> 00:03:22.050
and Matthew Turk, of course.

00:03:22.051 --> 00:03:24.080
So image correspondence,

00:03:24.081 --> 00:03:25.780
or solving this image
correspondence problem,

00:03:25.781 --> 00:03:27.600
is actually fundamental
in computer vision, right?

00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:29.950
So thanks to the solution
for that problem,

00:03:29.951 --> 00:03:34.385
we can actually reconstruct
large 3D models.

00:03:34.386 --> 00:03:36.260
We can actually
localize cameras if

00:03:36.261 --> 00:03:38.015
we have a 3D model, for example,

00:03:38.016 --> 00:03:40.035
or we can do image teaching,

00:03:40.036 --> 00:03:41.620
and many other applications.

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:43.330
So it's an important problem.

00:03:43.331 --> 00:03:46.105
As I mentioned before,

00:03:46.106 --> 00:03:49.155
solving the image
correspondence problem

00:03:49.156 --> 00:03:52.710
typically throws us data
that is not useful, okay?

00:03:52.711 --> 00:03:55.380
So when you actually get
the candidate correspondences,

00:03:55.381 --> 00:03:56.660
I will say that actually

00:03:56.661 --> 00:03:58.610
70 percent of them
are actually wrong.

00:03:58.611 --> 00:04:01.605
And so if we're lucky,
30 percent are actually good.

00:04:01.606 --> 00:04:06.270
Okay? And this is because
many things like rotation,

00:04:06.271 --> 00:04:07.810
elimination, scale variations

00:04:07.811 --> 00:04:09.390
in the process of the camera.

00:04:09.391 --> 00:04:13.800
So those factors actually
affect the feature matcher.

00:04:13.801 --> 00:04:19.215
So it basically
confuse it, okay?

00:04:19.216 --> 00:04:21.430
So the goal here,

00:04:21.431 --> 00:04:25.220
or one of the first goals
when I started my PhD was,

00:04:25.221 --> 00:04:27.600
"Can I actually predict which of

00:04:27.601 --> 00:04:30.260
those candidate
correspondences is true,

00:04:30.261 --> 00:04:32.590
or is a good one, based on

00:04:32.591 --> 00:04:33.910
the similarity scores that I

00:04:33.911 --> 00:04:35.925
get when I run
the feature match?"

00:04:35.926 --> 00:04:42.385
Okay? And to give you the
solution that I came up with,

00:04:42.386 --> 00:04:44.970
first, let me review, actually,

00:04:44.971 --> 00:04:47.830
how is it that
the feature match works?

00:04:47.831 --> 00:04:50.390
So imagine that I give
you these two images.

00:04:50.391 --> 00:04:52.770
I want to reconstruct say,
for example, the Colosseum.

00:04:52.771 --> 00:04:54.310
So the first thing
that you do is that

00:04:54.311 --> 00:04:55.590
you detect your features,

00:04:55.591 --> 00:04:57.480
which are visualized
with these circles,

00:04:57.481 --> 00:04:59.910
and then you basically

00:04:59.911 --> 00:05:01.750
forget about the context
of the image,

00:05:01.751 --> 00:05:04.580
where that feature
was located, for now.

00:05:04.581 --> 00:05:06.935
And then what you do
is that you only graph

00:05:06.936 --> 00:05:09.805
patches surrounding
that feature, okay?

00:05:09.806 --> 00:05:12.230
Then the next step is, say,

00:05:12.231 --> 00:05:13.320
for example, that
you want to find

00:05:13.321 --> 00:05:16.010
the feature matches
for this query feature.

00:05:16.011 --> 00:05:18.620
What you will do is that you

00:05:18.621 --> 00:05:21.900
will compare the representation,

00:05:21.901 --> 00:05:23.695
say, for example, SIFT or SURF,

00:05:23.696 --> 00:05:25.240
against all your reference

00:05:25.241 --> 00:05:26.860
features that you
have in your database.

00:05:26.861 --> 00:05:28.550
So basically this, this,

00:05:28.551 --> 00:05:29.740
and nearest neighbor
group, right?

00:05:29.741 --> 00:05:32.130
So you will have to, we
have to compute, say,

00:05:32.131 --> 00:05:35.210
a distance between
their SIFT descriptors.

00:05:35.211 --> 00:05:37.850
So you collect them, and then

00:05:37.851 --> 00:05:39.650
what you do is that
you basically grab

00:05:39.651 --> 00:05:41.890
the minimum and select

00:05:41.891 --> 00:05:43.970
or establish that correspondence

00:05:43.971 --> 00:05:45.140
following this rule, right?

00:05:45.141 --> 00:05:48.590
So essentially just selecting
the most similar feature,

00:05:48.591 --> 00:05:51.315
based on some appearance
representation.

00:05:51.316 --> 00:05:53.960
So these scores are going to be

00:05:53.961 --> 00:05:56.075
my random variables
of interest, okay?

00:05:56.076 --> 00:05:59.565
I want to predict,
based on this data,

00:05:59.566 --> 00:06:03.145
if the correspondence is
actually true or false.

00:06:03.146 --> 00:06:05.280
So this is actually

00:06:05.281 --> 00:06:07.160
the statistical model
that I came up with,

00:06:07.161 --> 00:06:09.330
and the main idea is as follows.

00:06:09.331 --> 00:06:11.960
So if I have

00:06:11.961 --> 00:06:15.310
a minimum distance for
a true correspondence,

00:06:15.311 --> 00:06:18.810
somehow that minimum distance
should be an outlier with

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:22.065
respect to the distances
of the false matches, okay?

00:06:22.066 --> 00:06:23.730
So if we have
a good representation,

00:06:23.731 --> 00:06:25.905
which in this case see if
there's a actually good one,

00:06:25.906 --> 00:06:28.185
it has proven to be a good one,

00:06:28.186 --> 00:06:31.455
then very likely
that score should look like

00:06:31.456 --> 00:06:33.430
an outlier with respect to

00:06:33.431 --> 00:06:35.950
the statistics of
the false matches, okay?

00:06:35.951 --> 00:06:38.890
So overall, you can actually
think about that there's

00:06:38.891 --> 00:06:42.615
an underlying process
with two distributions,

00:06:42.616 --> 00:06:45.240
one distribution
that is essentially

00:06:45.241 --> 00:06:48.050
drawing distances for
good correspondences,

00:06:48.051 --> 00:06:49.490
and another distribution that is

00:06:49.491 --> 00:06:51.595
giving you samples
for false matches.

00:06:51.596 --> 00:06:55.600
Okay? So when you
collect these distances,

00:06:55.601 --> 00:06:56.630
essentially when you run

00:06:56.631 --> 00:06:58.965
your feature matcher and
you collect this set.

00:06:58.966 --> 00:07:01.740
So it is equivalent,
or you can think about,

00:07:01.741 --> 00:07:05.210
getting that data set as
drawing from this mixture model,

00:07:05.211 --> 00:07:06.810
right? From these distributions.

00:07:06.811 --> 00:07:12.330
Now since I'm actually
considering a 3D vision problem,

00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:15.725
at most I can have
a correct correspondence, right?

00:07:15.726 --> 00:07:18.095
That's because
feature matching aims at

00:07:18.096 --> 00:07:19.990
identifying the same 3D point

00:07:19.991 --> 00:07:21.970
depicted at two images, okay?

00:07:21.971 --> 00:07:24.970
So at most I can have
one good solution.

00:07:24.971 --> 00:07:26.940
So this means that, this set,

00:07:26.941 --> 00:07:28.985
if I'm lucky and I have
a correct correspondence,

00:07:28.986 --> 00:07:32.370
I'm actually drawing one sample
from this green curve,

00:07:32.371 --> 00:07:33.690
which is representing,
in this case,

00:07:33.691 --> 00:07:35.115
a true correspondences.

00:07:35.116 --> 00:07:37.115
But then I actually
have a lot of

00:07:37.116 --> 00:07:40.670
false matching scores, right?

00:07:40.671 --> 00:07:42.770
Because there are other points,

00:07:42.771 --> 00:07:44.470
and therefore I'm going
to get scores that are

00:07:44.471 --> 00:07:47.015
actually coming from
this red distribution.

00:07:47.016 --> 00:07:50.925
So the goal here
is that somehow,

00:07:50.926 --> 00:07:53.365
what I want to do is

00:07:53.366 --> 00:07:57.660
model this course coming
from this distribution,

00:07:57.661 --> 00:08:00.990
because I actually have
more data for it, right?

00:08:00.991 --> 00:08:03.910
For example, if you want
to fit a parametric model,

00:08:03.911 --> 00:08:06.220
you know that you need
a lot of samples to

00:08:06.221 --> 00:08:07.260
actually get a good estimate of

00:08:07.261 --> 00:08:08.775
the parameters that
you want, right?

00:08:08.776 --> 00:08:10.730
But in this case, if I were

00:08:10.731 --> 00:08:14.420
to fit some model and try
to get some parameters,

00:08:14.421 --> 00:08:15.820
I only have at most one sample.

00:08:15.821 --> 00:08:18.315
So it's not going to be that
reliable for one sample.

00:08:18.316 --> 00:08:20.250
Whereas in this case,
from the false matches,

00:08:20.251 --> 00:08:21.970
it's reaching in data, right?

00:08:21.971 --> 00:08:23.290
As I explained before.

00:08:23.291 --> 00:08:25.125
So I can actually
work with this.

00:08:25.126 --> 00:08:26.410
Oh, you have a question.

00:08:26.411 --> 00:08:29.445
&gt;&gt; So is this the solution
for a single Y or infraction,

00:08:29.446 --> 00:08:35.965
do you otherwise do together?

00:08:35.966 --> 00:08:40.475
&gt;&gt; This is considered
only one query.

00:08:40.476 --> 00:08:42.320
So, meaning that I
have one query feature,

00:08:42.321 --> 00:08:43.640
and then I just
compare that query with

00:08:43.641 --> 00:08:46.590
all the rest. So
this is only for one.

00:08:46.591 --> 00:08:50.460
So rather than actually using

00:08:50.461 --> 00:08:53.120
the entire data that I have
here to try to come up,

00:08:53.121 --> 00:08:56.360
or estimate a distribution,

00:08:56.361 --> 00:08:57.725
promise of a distribution.

00:08:57.726 --> 00:09:00.370
What I'm going to do is
actually try to focus on

00:09:00.371 --> 00:09:01.790
the minimum distance that

00:09:01.791 --> 00:09:03.660
this process can
actually generate.

00:09:03.661 --> 00:09:07.135
And that's because
the nearest neighbor matter,

00:09:07.136 --> 00:09:09.380
it only focuses on
the minimum distance, right?

00:09:09.381 --> 00:09:11.940
So the rule is
telling it to only

00:09:11.941 --> 00:09:13.590
decide based on
the minimum distance

00:09:13.591 --> 00:09:15.460
that you get out
of the sampling.

00:09:15.461 --> 00:09:18.955
So, the goal is into model,

00:09:18.956 --> 00:09:20.220
what is the minimum distance

00:09:20.221 --> 00:09:21.760
that these process can generate?

00:09:21.761 --> 00:09:23.245
And for that actually,

00:09:23.246 --> 00:09:25.505
there are statistical
theories that give you

00:09:25.506 --> 00:09:28.365
the distribution that
models that behavior.

00:09:28.366 --> 00:09:31.210
So once I have that model of

00:09:31.211 --> 00:09:34.785
the minimum distance coming from
these [inaudible] process, then,

00:09:34.786 --> 00:09:38.140
I can actually do a test,

00:09:38.141 --> 00:09:40.000
and check if the
minimum distance that I

00:09:40.001 --> 00:09:43.140
have agrees with that model
that I just computed,

00:09:43.141 --> 00:09:45.830
if so then, I can actually
predict that it is false.

00:09:45.831 --> 00:09:47.825
Otherwise, it's actually a good.

00:09:47.826 --> 00:09:49.430
That's the whole idea
of a research.

00:09:49.431 --> 00:09:53.295
&gt;&gt; Do we expect the false
correspondent matches

00:09:53.296 --> 00:09:55.250
towards the [inaudible]

00:09:55.251 --> 00:09:58.720
&gt;&gt; Oh, that's actually
a good question.

00:09:58.721 --> 00:10:00.070
So this is just an illustration.

00:10:00.071 --> 00:10:03.050
I'm not assuming a Gaussian,
or anything like that.

00:10:03.051 --> 00:10:05.910
This is just the state that
I have two distributions,

00:10:05.911 --> 00:10:08.685
they may have weird
shapes, and so forth.

00:10:08.686 --> 00:10:10.100
This is just to illustrate that

00:10:10.101 --> 00:10:12.000
I am considering
two processes here.

00:10:12.001 --> 00:10:16.610
Yeah. So, what I'm

00:10:16.611 --> 00:10:18.770
going to use here is
something that is called

00:10:18.771 --> 00:10:22.800
Extreme Value Theory It's
a statistical theory that deals

00:10:22.801 --> 00:10:27.330
with maximum or minimum
of a random process.

00:10:27.331 --> 00:10:29.480
Essentially, it aims to

00:10:29.481 --> 00:10:32.350
give you the distributions
that will model,

00:10:32.351 --> 00:10:35.860
either the largest scores
coming from a random process,

00:10:35.861 --> 00:10:38.085
or the minimum score
from a random process.

00:10:38.086 --> 00:10:40.350
And in particular
for this problem,

00:10:40.351 --> 00:10:42.560
the theorem that works the best

00:10:42.561 --> 00:10:45.415
is something that is called
the Block Maxima theorem.

00:10:45.416 --> 00:10:48.710
It turns out in the linear,

00:10:48.711 --> 00:10:50.050
you can proof that

00:10:50.051 --> 00:10:51.900
the distribution that
models the minimum,

00:10:51.901 --> 00:10:54.170
or the maximum is a distribution

00:10:54.171 --> 00:10:56.795
called the Generalized
Extreme Value distribution.

00:10:56.796 --> 00:11:00.185
So, it is actually
a very complex distribution,

00:11:00.186 --> 00:11:01.790
but it is so

00:11:01.791 --> 00:11:04.250
general that in the linear
it actually holds.

00:11:04.251 --> 00:11:05.810
And according to
the theory it hold

00:11:05.811 --> 00:11:08.355
for a lot of processes.

00:11:08.356 --> 00:11:11.160
And it has special cases.

00:11:11.161 --> 00:11:13.200
So one of those
special cases that

00:11:13.201 --> 00:11:14.290
I'll be using a lot is actually

00:11:14.291 --> 00:11:15.540
the Rayleigh distribution,

00:11:15.541 --> 00:11:17.520
but others that are
common for example,

00:11:17.521 --> 00:11:20.245
are Weibull,
Frechet, and Gumble.

00:11:20.246 --> 00:11:24.285
So, just to give you an example
of how this theory works,

00:11:24.286 --> 00:11:28.410
imagine that I have a normal
distribution in one [inaudible].

00:11:28.411 --> 00:11:31.590
And then, imagine
this experiment.

00:11:31.591 --> 00:11:34.470
So, I'm going to draw 10,000

00:11:34.471 --> 00:11:37.795
samples from these
Gaussian distribution,

00:11:37.796 --> 00:11:39.705
and then you keep
the minimum of that.

00:11:39.706 --> 00:11:41.890
Then you draw another

00:11:41.891 --> 00:11:44.630
10,000 samples from
the same distribution,

00:11:44.631 --> 00:11:46.560
and you keep again
the minimum of that.

00:11:46.561 --> 00:11:48.430
So you repeat that process

00:11:48.431 --> 00:11:50.125
for that experiment
multiple times,

00:11:50.126 --> 00:11:52.845
saving the minimum score
that you got.

00:11:52.846 --> 00:11:55.370
The distribution
that is going to

00:11:55.371 --> 00:11:58.015
model those minimums
is actually the GEV,

00:11:58.016 --> 00:11:59.310
or the generalized extreme value

