00:17:43.381 --> 00:17:46.715
you will get like
1000 features or 2000 features.

00:17:46.716 --> 00:17:48.840
Of course, depending on
the scene that you're seeing.

00:17:48.841 --> 00:17:51.945
If it's wall texture, you
will have a lot of features.

00:17:51.946 --> 00:17:55.610
But if it's a wall
with no texture at all,

00:17:55.611 --> 00:17:56.985
probably you will have a few.

00:17:56.986 --> 00:18:00.720
But yes, this is run
per every feature.

00:18:00.721 --> 00:18:04.995
This is the algorithm.
It's fairly simple.

00:18:04.996 --> 00:18:09.820
The journalistic
values distribution

00:18:09.821 --> 00:18:14.400
has some parameters that
are actually hard to fit.

00:18:14.401 --> 00:18:17.315
It turns out that
the maximum likelihood estimator

00:18:17.316 --> 00:18:20.745
has some nice properties only
under certain conditions.

00:18:20.746 --> 00:18:23.520
But then if you don't
comply with it then there's

00:18:23.521 --> 00:18:26.300
no guarantee of
maximum likelihood at all.

00:18:26.301 --> 00:18:28.305
In order to avoid
any issues with it,

00:18:28.306 --> 00:18:29.590
we decided to use

00:18:29.591 --> 00:18:30.800
a special case of

00:18:30.801 --> 00:18:32.565
that which is
the Rayleigh distribution.

00:18:32.566 --> 00:18:36.165
It only has one parameter
which is the scale.

00:18:36.166 --> 00:18:41.485
That totally minimize those
issues that I was explaining.

00:18:41.486 --> 00:18:43.950
I'm going to show you

00:18:43.951 --> 00:18:47.070
some experiments that I
did with some results.

00:18:47.071 --> 00:18:50.265
For this experiment, I
used the dataset that is

00:18:50.266 --> 00:18:53.820
widely used in the community.

00:18:53.821 --> 00:18:56.810
It is called the Oxford
Affine Covariant dataset.

00:18:56.811 --> 00:19:00.690
It's giving you basically

00:19:00.691 --> 00:19:02.450
a bunch of images and

00:19:02.451 --> 00:19:04.890
for every image, you
have homographies.

00:19:04.891 --> 00:19:07.140
Remember, the homography
transformation pixel to

00:19:07.141 --> 00:19:10.415
pixel from image
one to image two.

00:19:10.416 --> 00:19:12.520
They give you this ground truth.

00:19:12.521 --> 00:19:15.720
You can actually get
or determine what

00:19:15.721 --> 00:19:17.500
are the right correspondences

00:19:17.501 --> 00:19:18.705
when you do this experiment.

00:19:18.706 --> 00:19:20.125
Thanks to this homography.

00:19:20.126 --> 00:19:22.210
You can actually
run your matching

00:19:22.211 --> 00:19:24.140
method and then thanks
to the [inaudible]

00:19:24.141 --> 00:19:28.560
define which correspond
to [inaudible].

00:19:28.561 --> 00:19:31.025
These are actually ROC plots.

00:19:31.026 --> 00:19:34.840
True positives on the y axis
meaning that how good

00:19:34.841 --> 00:19:36.560
my predictor worked

00:19:36.561 --> 00:19:39.625
determining the right
correspondences.

00:19:39.626 --> 00:19:41.460
On the X axis, we have

00:19:41.461 --> 00:19:45.320
false positive rate meaning
the rate of false alarms.

00:19:45.321 --> 00:19:48.230
Meaning that if the classifier

00:19:48.231 --> 00:19:51.310
thought it was correct
but in reality it was not.

00:19:51.311 --> 00:19:55.520
Clearly what we want
is a point that is

00:19:55.521 --> 00:19:58.410
high in true positive rate

00:19:58.411 --> 00:20:00.445
and minimum in
false positive rate.

00:20:00.446 --> 00:20:03.330
In order to get this curve,

00:20:03.331 --> 00:20:06.060
you vary the threshold

00:20:06.061 --> 00:20:10.045
from zero to
maybe some high number.

00:20:10.046 --> 00:20:12.430
For every threshold
that you have.

00:20:12.431 --> 00:20:14.005
you have a point in this curve.

00:20:14.006 --> 00:20:15.410
Okay. I'm actually violating

00:20:15.411 --> 00:20:18.135
several thresholds at the same.

00:20:18.136 --> 00:20:19.810
These are a couple
of baselines and I'm

00:20:19.811 --> 00:20:22.840
comparing the proposed approach.

00:20:22.841 --> 00:20:24.630
The proposed approach
is actually.

00:20:24.631 --> 00:20:27.740
the red curve. We named
this male recognition Rayleigh.

00:20:27.741 --> 00:20:32.010
On the left we have
for sift features,

00:20:32.011 --> 00:20:34.565
and on the right we
have for surf features.

00:20:34.566 --> 00:20:39.020
The main baseline here
is what I call lowest

00:20:39.021 --> 00:20:40.835
ratio but also in the community

00:20:40.836 --> 00:20:43.200
it is known as
the Sift Ratio Test.

00:20:43.201 --> 00:20:46.275
That test is very simple.

00:20:46.276 --> 00:20:48.090
When you do your matching,

00:20:48.091 --> 00:20:49.320
you get your distances.

00:20:49.321 --> 00:20:52.150
You divide the smallest distance

00:20:52.151 --> 00:20:54.335
and the second
smallest distance.

00:20:54.336 --> 00:20:56.690
That ratio is widely used in

00:20:56.691 --> 00:21:00.255
the community to actually
filter about correspondences.

00:21:00.256 --> 00:21:04.160
Then I used
another baseline just

00:21:04.161 --> 00:21:07.530
to compare something that
is called the Brown's Ratio.

00:21:07.531 --> 00:21:09.000
It was a paper CDPR.

00:21:09.001 --> 00:21:13.380
Very similar to the sift
ratio test but instead of

00:21:13.381 --> 00:21:16.940
dividing the smallest distance

00:21:16.941 --> 00:21:18.680
with respect to the second one,

00:21:18.681 --> 00:21:21.750
we take the average of
the second one and the third as

00:21:21.751 --> 00:21:25.900
a denominator and then we
again compute the ratio.

00:21:25.901 --> 00:21:27.735
That is the black curve.

00:21:27.736 --> 00:21:31.990
There's another method that
is very similar to the one

00:21:31.991 --> 00:21:33.865
proposed but it was proposed

00:21:33.866 --> 00:21:37.095
mainly for visual
classification.

00:21:37.096 --> 00:21:39.830
What they found
in a PAMI paper is

00:21:39.831 --> 00:21:40.990
that the Weibull distribution at

00:21:40.991 --> 00:21:42.500
least for those problems,

00:21:42.501 --> 00:21:45.740
work well in another domain.

00:21:45.741 --> 00:21:47.550
Here I'm showing that
the Rayleigh distribution

00:21:47.551 --> 00:21:50.640
for future matching
actually works well.

00:21:50.641 --> 00:21:54.360
As you can see here,
the red curve is above

00:21:54.361 --> 00:21:56.800
the other methods which

00:21:56.801 --> 00:21:59.515
means that this seems
to be working okay.

00:21:59.516 --> 00:22:00.850
On average, you will see

00:22:00.851 --> 00:22:02.925
an improvement of
three to five per cent.

00:22:02.926 --> 00:22:04.960
If you just want
to predict through

00:22:04.961 --> 00:22:08.360
correspondences by just using

00:22:08.361 --> 00:22:11.140
the similarity score
of the six matches.

00:22:11.141 --> 00:22:15.025
Okay. This turned out
to be really good.

00:22:15.026 --> 00:22:18.770
The next problem that I
wanted to tackle given

00:22:18.771 --> 00:22:22.790
this was to speed up
all the estimations that occur.

00:22:22.791 --> 00:22:24.485
For example, in
structure for motion.

00:22:24.486 --> 00:22:27.035
Once you have
these correspondences,

00:22:27.036 --> 00:22:30.070
at the end of the day when
I get a 3D model or maybe

00:22:30.071 --> 00:22:33.155
estimate a homography
or a fundamental matrix,

00:22:33.156 --> 00:22:34.670
in order to do other things,

00:22:34.671 --> 00:22:36.860
to understand for example
the relative motion

00:22:36.861 --> 00:22:38.425
of cameras and so forth,

00:22:38.426 --> 00:22:40.495
in order to do that,

00:22:40.496 --> 00:22:45.400
we actually have to run
something that's called RANSAC.

00:22:45.401 --> 00:22:49.520
RANSAC can be slow
especially if we

00:22:49.521 --> 00:22:52.640
pass an image pair that

00:22:52.641 --> 00:22:54.590
has only a few correspondences

00:22:54.591 --> 00:22:55.950
that are correct
say for example,

00:22:55.951 --> 00:22:57.030
five per cent or one per

00:22:57.031 --> 00:22:58.730
cent, it's going
to take forever.

00:22:58.731 --> 00:23:01.025
Just to remind you about RANSAC.

00:23:01.026 --> 00:23:04.230
RANSAC is this iterative
algorithm where you have

00:23:04.231 --> 00:23:06.580
your candidate
correspondences in

00:23:06.581 --> 00:23:08.800
our domain but it's
actually more general.

00:23:08.801 --> 00:23:11.420
You select randomly a subset of

00:23:11.421 --> 00:23:15.080
the data then based on
that subset you fit a model.

00:23:15.081 --> 00:23:17.075
Basically, you get a hypothesis.

00:23:17.076 --> 00:23:21.310
The next step is to check
with the data itself and say,

00:23:21.311 --> 00:23:24.845
"How many points in my dataset
agree with the model that

00:23:24.846 --> 00:23:28.695
this hypothesis
that I just drew?"

00:23:28.696 --> 00:23:31.080
You repeat this and you

00:23:31.081 --> 00:23:33.040
always keep, it's like
a greedy algorithm.

00:23:33.041 --> 00:23:35.340
You always keep the one that
is explaining the most of

00:23:35.341 --> 00:23:40.300
your day of the kind
of correspondences.

00:23:40.301 --> 00:23:42.420
Once you reach
a number of iterations,

00:23:42.421 --> 00:23:45.235
you return the best model.

