00:36:23.441 --> 00:36:26.380
So, for example, this first row
is using SURF features,

00:36:26.381 --> 00:36:29.025
then the second row
is SIFT features.

00:36:29.026 --> 00:36:31.970
And here is the number of
correspondences that I got.

00:36:31.971 --> 00:36:33.100
And then this Epsilon is

00:36:33.101 --> 00:36:35.725
the ground truth
for that image pair.

00:36:35.726 --> 00:36:37.750
So this means that
only one percent of

00:36:37.751 --> 00:36:39.450
the candidate correspondence was

00:36:39.451 --> 00:36:41.190
correct according
to the ground truth.

00:36:41.191 --> 00:36:42.825
And I saw them, okay?

00:36:42.826 --> 00:36:48.030
So, the lowest inlier ratio
to the highest inlier ratio.

00:36:48.031 --> 00:36:50.050
So these are too many numbers,

00:36:50.051 --> 00:36:51.170
but I want you to focus on

00:36:51.171 --> 00:36:54.345
these boxes that
I'm highlighting.

00:36:54.346 --> 00:36:55.920
So, what you can see here,

00:36:55.921 --> 00:36:58.695
for example, consider
the first row.

00:36:58.696 --> 00:37:03.040
Models means the number of
hypotheses that the algorithm

00:37:03.041 --> 00:37:07.310
had to draw in order to
reach to the good solution.

00:37:07.311 --> 00:37:09.490
And then time here
is just milliseconds,

00:37:09.491 --> 00:37:10.530
the number of
milliseconds that it

00:37:10.531 --> 00:37:12.140
took to actually convert.

00:37:12.141 --> 00:37:14.680
So, what you can see
here is that actually,

00:37:14.681 --> 00:37:17.300
EVSAC thanks to this model,

00:37:17.301 --> 00:37:19.820
it only took 4.2

00:37:19.821 --> 00:37:22.675
milliseconds to actually
reach to a solution.

00:37:22.676 --> 00:37:26.495
Compared to for example, PROSAC
which is actually faster,

00:37:26.496 --> 00:37:29.100
but the number of good runs

00:37:29.101 --> 00:37:33.280
means that out of
the 300 trials that I got,

00:37:33.281 --> 00:37:36.150
EVSAC, 100 percent of the time

00:37:36.151 --> 00:37:38.645
returned the same answer,
which was good.

00:37:38.646 --> 00:37:40.090
But PROSAC only,

00:37:40.091 --> 00:37:42.860
less than one percent
return a good solution.

00:37:42.861 --> 00:37:46.575
Then the remaining results
were completely wrong.

00:37:46.576 --> 00:37:48.770
So this means that EVSAC

00:37:48.771 --> 00:37:50.830
tends to be more
consistent than others.

00:37:50.831 --> 00:37:52.430
And of course, in this case,

00:37:52.431 --> 00:37:54.400
RANSAC took forever
and never converts,

00:37:54.401 --> 00:37:56.495
and so, never return
a good solution.

00:37:56.496 --> 00:38:00.800
You'll see the same trend
as you go down.

00:38:00.801 --> 00:38:04.200
So, EVSAC is very

00:38:04.201 --> 00:38:07.605
competitive especially when
the inlier ratio is very low.

00:38:07.606 --> 00:38:11.820
But when you start getting
a higher inlier ratio,

00:38:11.821 --> 00:38:14.295
meaning that you have
more good correspondences,

00:38:14.296 --> 00:38:16.060
then EVSAC tends to behave

00:38:16.061 --> 00:38:18.280
on par with
the competing methods.

00:38:18.281 --> 00:38:20.675
Okay? So the real
advantage here is,

00:38:20.676 --> 00:38:23.760
when you have an image pair that

00:38:23.761 --> 00:38:27.510
is not that well behaved
in terms of that,

00:38:27.511 --> 00:38:33.350
we don't have
the many inliers, okay?

00:38:33.351 --> 00:38:37.060
Now, that actually
comprise the part of what

00:38:37.061 --> 00:38:40.370
I did or what I tackled
as part of my PhD.

00:38:40.371 --> 00:38:43.110
And then I'm going to talk
about some other topics

00:38:43.111 --> 00:38:45.650
that I'm recently working.

00:38:45.651 --> 00:38:47.450
And these are actually results

00:38:47.451 --> 00:38:49.285
that I haven't
published yet formally,

00:38:49.286 --> 00:38:52.465
but I just want to show
where I'm going and

00:38:52.466 --> 00:38:56.320
what I'm thinking in
terms of research now.

00:38:56.321 --> 00:38:58.500
So, I'm going to
show an approach

00:38:58.501 --> 00:39:00.580
to learn from
a long-tail dataset,

00:39:00.581 --> 00:39:02.140
and this is work that I did with

00:39:02.141 --> 00:39:05.350
Deva Ramanan from
CMU last summer,

00:39:05.351 --> 00:39:08.100
although we are
still working on it.

00:39:08.101 --> 00:39:10.850
The motivation came
from this picture.

00:39:10.851 --> 00:39:13.790
He's very excited about
the Open-World Problem.

00:39:13.791 --> 00:39:15.530
And then I got
excited about it as

00:39:15.531 --> 00:39:18.825
well because it deals
with the rare data.

00:39:18.826 --> 00:39:21.660
Clearly, the problem
if you actually train

00:39:21.661 --> 00:39:24.340
say a network
using this dataset,

00:39:24.341 --> 00:39:27.105
very likely, the network
is going to bias, right?

00:39:27.106 --> 00:39:28.520
It's going to do very well for

00:39:28.521 --> 00:39:30.110
the classes that have
a lot of samples,

00:39:30.111 --> 00:39:31.605
but it's going to do poorly for

00:39:31.606 --> 00:39:34.480
these concepts that
only have a few samples.

00:39:34.481 --> 00:39:35.810
So, this problem is actually

00:39:35.811 --> 00:39:37.870
different from
a fissure learning.

00:39:37.871 --> 00:39:40.110
Because here, we
are actually trying

00:39:40.111 --> 00:39:42.815
to tackle both problems
at the same time.

00:39:42.816 --> 00:39:45.210
So you have concepts that
have a lot of samples,

00:39:45.211 --> 00:39:46.880
but others that only have few.

00:39:46.881 --> 00:39:49.250
So how do make

00:39:49.251 --> 00:39:50.900
the network world behave

00:39:50.901 --> 00:39:53.165
well for both of them
at the same time?

00:39:53.166 --> 00:39:55.875
Now, also the community hasn't,

00:39:55.876 --> 00:39:58.995
or doesn't have
a good evaluation protocol.

00:39:58.996 --> 00:40:01.670
So, another contribution
that we believe is

00:40:01.671 --> 00:40:05.850
useful is a protocol that
we're proposing here.

00:40:05.851 --> 00:40:08.730
Some previous work on
that that are tackling

00:40:08.731 --> 00:40:12.080
this can be categorized
as follows: So,

00:40:12.081 --> 00:40:15.050
sampling-based solutions
essentially say, well,

00:40:15.051 --> 00:40:17.935
if you have this
unbalanced datasets,

00:40:17.936 --> 00:40:19.020
well try to balance it.

00:40:19.021 --> 00:40:20.450
So you can probably undersample

00:40:20.451 --> 00:40:22.605
some classes and
try to come up with

00:40:22.606 --> 00:40:24.295
a training set that has

00:40:24.296 --> 00:40:27.485
fairly the same number
of training instances.

00:40:27.486 --> 00:40:29.575
The middle learning approaches,

00:40:29.576 --> 00:40:31.090
they are actually
very interesting.

00:40:31.091 --> 00:40:33.140
So what they do in
a CNN is that they

00:40:33.141 --> 00:40:35.710
train another module that

00:40:35.711 --> 00:40:38.445
predicts the grading updates

00:40:38.446 --> 00:40:41.605
for the classes that
have only a few samples.

00:40:41.606 --> 00:40:44.490
Okay? And then
some other problems

00:40:44.491 --> 00:40:48.195
that are actually very
related, fissure learning.

00:40:48.196 --> 00:40:51.130
Well, these are actually
fairly recent but each of

00:40:51.131 --> 00:40:53.160
them is very different.

00:40:53.161 --> 00:40:55.640
And then cost-based,
essentially,

00:40:55.641 --> 00:40:59.760
is imposing penalties in
the optimization problem, right?

00:40:59.761 --> 00:41:02.780
It's going to penalize
the classifier if it's actually

00:41:02.781 --> 00:41:04.425
doing a poor job

00:41:04.426 --> 00:41:06.675
for classes that are
not well represented.

00:41:06.676 --> 00:41:09.825
So what we're proposing
is something very simple.

00:41:09.826 --> 00:41:12.550
We wanted to come up with
something simple because that's

00:41:12.551 --> 00:41:16.130
what people adopt
more easily, right?

00:41:16.131 --> 00:41:18.230
So the idea was, yes?

00:41:18.231 --> 00:41:19.750
&gt;&gt; A question, sir. In
all of these examples,

00:41:19.751 --> 00:41:21.750
you have images and labels,

00:41:21.751 --> 00:41:23.010
except that you don't
have a lot of [inaudible]

00:41:23.011 --> 00:41:24.480
&gt;&gt; Yes exactly.

00:41:24.481 --> 00:41:25.550
So yes, this picture.

00:41:25.551 --> 00:41:26.915
&gt;&gt; There is no unlabeled data?

00:41:26.916 --> 00:41:28.580
&gt;&gt; Yes, there's no unlabeled.

00:41:28.581 --> 00:41:30.260
Everything's labeled
except that you

00:41:30.261 --> 00:41:33.245
have for some more than others.

00:41:33.246 --> 00:41:34.995
So what we propose is,

00:41:34.996 --> 00:41:36.585
can we learn on embedding

00:41:36.586 --> 00:41:41.260
that allows us to deal
with this thing on balance?

00:41:41.261 --> 00:41:42.970
And the idea came

00:41:42.971 --> 00:41:46.585
up mainly from
a Gaussian mixture model.

00:41:46.586 --> 00:41:48.175
If you think of

00:41:48.176 --> 00:41:50.290
every single concept
that you want to learn,

00:41:50.291 --> 00:41:51.520
and then it's represented from

00:41:51.521 --> 00:41:54.665
a single Gaussian distribution.

00:41:54.666 --> 00:41:57.890
You remember that in
the Gaussian mixture model

00:41:57.891 --> 00:41:59.720
you have this priors
that somehow are

00:41:59.721 --> 00:42:01.525
encoding or can encode

00:42:01.526 --> 00:42:04.095
the imbalance in
the dataset, right?

00:42:04.096 --> 00:42:06.130
So the idea is to
have that model to

00:42:06.131 --> 00:42:08.610
then take that out of
the equation and see if

00:42:08.611 --> 00:42:11.120
the classifier will do

00:42:11.121 --> 00:42:15.515
better if we somehow twiggered
to ignore that imbalance.

00:42:15.516 --> 00:42:18.130
So, the idea of
these embedding is actually to

00:42:18.131 --> 00:42:21.085
represent each concept
with a single centroid.

00:42:21.086 --> 00:42:22.740
And hopefully, the network will

00:42:22.741 --> 00:42:27.200
learn a function that minimizes

00:42:27.201 --> 00:42:29.885
the inter-class variation
so that we can

00:42:29.886 --> 00:42:31.780
have a compact representation

00:42:31.781 --> 00:42:33.765
for all the instances per class.

00:42:33.766 --> 00:42:35.260
So here, I'm just giving you

00:42:35.261 --> 00:42:37.955
an example of how this works.

00:42:37.956 --> 00:42:43.450
So, cats are very popular
on the web. Cars as well.

00:42:43.451 --> 00:42:46.945
But when you search
for a white reindeer,

00:42:46.946 --> 00:42:50.450
it's very unlikely that you
will find many pictures.

00:42:50.451 --> 00:42:53.740
So, that's the overall idea.

00:42:53.741 --> 00:42:57.040
And one of the benefits
that we thought this

00:42:57.041 --> 00:42:58.260
could have is the

00:42:58.261 --> 00:43:00.700
following: compared
two linear classifiers,

00:43:00.701 --> 00:43:02.330
you have your linear
classifier and

00:43:02.331 --> 00:43:03.810
you have a new
training instance,

00:43:03.811 --> 00:43:06.420
say, for example,
falls in this region,

00:43:06.421 --> 00:43:08.110
then very likely,
this new instance

00:43:08.111 --> 00:43:09.570
is not going to
give you an update

00:43:09.571 --> 00:43:10.780
of your parameters because it's

00:43:10.781 --> 00:43:13.125
already classified correctly.

00:43:13.126 --> 00:43:15.095
But when you add
another instance

00:43:15.096 --> 00:43:17.360
using this
center-based approach,

00:43:17.361 --> 00:43:19.705
then very likely,
that center has to move.

00:43:19.706 --> 00:43:22.720
Okay? So, it is actually
very sensitive to that.

00:43:22.721 --> 00:43:24.500
And we think that this

00:43:24.501 --> 00:43:26.250
is actually a benefit
of our approach.

00:43:26.251 --> 00:43:28.720
So, in order to learn
these embedding,

00:43:28.721 --> 00:43:31.360
we proposed this
optimization function.

00:43:31.361 --> 00:43:35.645
So, classical
cross entropy loss,

00:43:35.646 --> 00:43:37.640
where we have the embeddings and

00:43:37.641 --> 00:43:40.065
w are the parameters
of the network.

00:43:40.066 --> 00:43:42.280
And then we have the centers,

00:43:42.281 --> 00:43:44.320
one center per class.

00:43:44.321 --> 00:43:48.110
And then if you only consider
this part of the equation,

00:43:48.111 --> 00:43:49.840
essentially, is just encoding

00:43:49.841 --> 00:43:51.425
the newest class
mean classifier.

00:43:51.426 --> 00:43:54.245
Which is very similar
to the nearest neighbor.

00:43:54.246 --> 00:43:58.375
It's just going to assign
the class to the closest mean.

00:43:58.376 --> 00:44:00.830
And then, we added a regularizer

00:44:00.831 --> 00:44:04.470
here just to make sure
that these centers

00:44:04.471 --> 00:44:07.330
which are actually
optimized or learn

00:44:07.331 --> 00:44:11.935
using regular deep
learning methods,

00:44:11.936 --> 00:44:15.090
just so that it complies
with the statistics of

00:44:15.091 --> 00:44:18.770
the embedding as you run
your optimization process.

00:44:18.771 --> 00:44:21.490
So here, this term is

00:44:21.491 --> 00:44:22.600
just grabbing from the mini

00:44:22.601 --> 00:44:23.940
batches computing
the center from

00:44:23.941 --> 00:44:26.740
the mini batch and then
enforcing that the estimates are

00:44:26.741 --> 00:44:28.500
close to the center

00:44:28.501 --> 00:44:30.820
that is being estimated from
the deep learning framework.

00:44:30.821 --> 00:44:33.765
So this is the objective
function that we proposed.

00:44:33.766 --> 00:44:36.400
As you see, it has

00:44:36.401 --> 00:44:38.190
some relationship with
linear classifier which

00:44:38.191 --> 00:44:40.520
makes it easy to implement.

00:44:40.521 --> 00:44:42.670
And the relationship is
the following: If you

00:44:42.671 --> 00:44:45.635
expand this quadratic term,

00:44:45.636 --> 00:44:47.510
you will get this,

00:44:47.511 --> 00:44:49.290
and then as also you directly

00:44:49.291 --> 00:44:51.335
establish a relationship with

00:44:51.336 --> 00:44:56.125
regular softmax classifiers
with the exception though,

00:44:56.126 --> 00:44:59.060
is with this bias over here.

00:44:59.061 --> 00:45:01.975
This bias is actually
constrained by these terms.

00:45:01.976 --> 00:45:05.230
So, the bias is actually half of

00:45:05.231 --> 00:45:08.460
the center of the class
that you have at hand.

00:45:08.461 --> 00:45:09.690
This term actually can be

00:45:09.691 --> 00:45:11.425
factored out and
then cancels out.

00:45:11.426 --> 00:45:13.435
So, the relationship with

00:45:13.436 --> 00:45:16.550
linear classifier
is that essentially,

00:45:16.551 --> 00:45:18.880
we're fitting linear classifiers

00:45:18.881 --> 00:45:22.565
where the w
represents the center,

00:45:22.566 --> 00:45:25.160
and then the bias section
restricted by the norm

00:45:25.161 --> 00:45:28.655
of the center or
the norm of the center.

00:45:28.656 --> 00:45:32.710
This is useful for
implementation details.

00:45:32.711 --> 00:45:36.505
Now, in terms of
the evaluation protocol,

00:45:36.506 --> 00:45:40.140
what we propose is
the following approach.

00:45:40.141 --> 00:45:43.470
So, we have
the imbalance dataset,

00:45:43.471 --> 00:45:46.980
and what we want is actually
to capture and see if

00:45:46.981 --> 00:45:48.810
the classifier generalizes well

00:45:48.811 --> 00:45:51.425
for classes that are
not well represented.

00:45:51.426 --> 00:45:53.920
So, what we proposed is to

00:45:53.921 --> 00:45:57.875
actually sample the
training set multiple times,

00:45:57.876 --> 00:45:59.490
and then for every sample,

00:45:59.491 --> 00:46:01.225
train a different model.

00:46:01.226 --> 00:46:03.765
Now, that I have those models,

00:46:03.766 --> 00:46:07.130
I'm going to use the balance
set in order to make

00:46:07.131 --> 00:46:08.760
sure that the
classifier is actually

00:46:08.761 --> 00:46:10.765
generalizing given
this different samples.

00:46:10.766 --> 00:46:14.530
This is just of measure
and see if I have

00:46:14.531 --> 00:46:19.790
a class variation for some
concepts, can this generalize?

00:46:19.791 --> 00:46:24.280
And then normally,

00:46:24.281 --> 00:46:26.160
you just plot the report

00:46:26.161 --> 00:46:27.780
of the per-class
accuracy average.

00:46:27.781 --> 00:46:30.145
So, out of those trials
that you had,

00:46:30.146 --> 00:46:33.865
report just the average for
those trials for every class.

00:46:33.866 --> 00:46:36.130
That's what we proposed
in comparison with

00:46:36.131 --> 00:46:38.710
other methods which
are just train,

00:46:38.711 --> 00:46:40.720
and then evaluate
using as if you

00:46:40.721 --> 00:46:43.770
had a balanced dataset,
which may not be right.

00:46:43.771 --> 00:46:49.645
So, here are some results
on small-scale datasets.

00:46:49.646 --> 00:46:53.135
I compare this with
other center-based approaches.

00:46:53.136 --> 00:47:00.490
One that was recently published
at CVPR 2017 was iCARL.

00:47:00.491 --> 00:47:03.935
Essentially, iCARL graphs
are soft Max,

00:47:03.936 --> 00:47:05.540
computes all the features

00:47:05.541 --> 00:47:06.900
for all the classes
that you have,

00:47:06.901 --> 00:47:08.070
and then it computes the center

00:47:08.071 --> 00:47:09.735
for every class, and that's it.

00:47:09.736 --> 00:47:12.925
So, there are two versions:
one that is on normalized,

00:47:12.926 --> 00:47:14.530
which is what I just described,

00:47:14.531 --> 00:47:16.420
and then normalized means

00:47:16.421 --> 00:47:19.715
that all the vectors that
you are dealing with,

00:47:19.716 --> 00:47:20.880
they have to be normalized.

00:47:20.881 --> 00:47:24.700
And the reason that they
used it, according to them,

00:47:24.701 --> 00:47:26.710
is that actually helps reduce

00:47:26.711 --> 00:47:29.435
some noise effects in the data.

00:47:29.436 --> 00:47:32.705
Then I just used
the regular GMM approach.

00:47:32.706 --> 00:47:34.960
After I have the
Softmax representation,

00:47:34.961 --> 00:47:36.990
I fit a GMM,

00:47:36.991 --> 00:47:39.290
and that's it. Those
are the baselines.

00:47:39.291 --> 00:47:41.005
So, on the right,

00:47:41.006 --> 00:47:43.955
you will see the accuracy,

00:47:43.956 --> 00:47:48.510
the average of
the Softmax classifier.

00:47:48.511 --> 00:47:50.645
And as you can see here,
for example, for MNIST,

00:47:50.646 --> 00:47:52.390
you know that MNIST is
actually a balanced dataset,

00:47:52.391 --> 00:47:53.780
I actually had to
long-tail them,

00:47:53.781 --> 00:47:55.560
to make it imbalance.

00:47:55.561 --> 00:47:57.860
So, as the index grows,

00:47:57.861 --> 00:48:00.875
the imbalance growth
here in the plot.

00:48:00.876 --> 00:48:02.395
So, as you can see here,

00:48:02.396 --> 00:48:03.880
the average or the accuracy

00:48:03.881 --> 00:48:06.635
drops when that
imbalance occurs.

00:48:06.636 --> 00:48:08.790
This shaded region is

00:48:08.791 --> 00:48:12.725
just the standard deviation
that I got from the trials.

00:48:12.726 --> 00:48:15.070
Now, the plot on the left

00:48:15.071 --> 00:48:17.425
is just showing
the relative performance.

00:48:17.426 --> 00:48:20.840
That means the accuracy
that I got from every of

00:48:20.841 --> 00:48:22.140
these methods divided by

00:48:22.141 --> 00:48:24.875
the accuracy that I got
from the Softmax classifier.

00:48:24.876 --> 00:48:26.190
This is just to clearly

00:48:26.191 --> 00:48:27.855
see if there's
an improvement or not.

00:48:27.856 --> 00:48:29.345
Where there's an improvement,

00:48:29.346 --> 00:48:31.155
you get something
larger than one.

00:48:31.156 --> 00:48:32.700
If there's a drop,

00:48:32.701 --> 00:48:35.155
you will see points
below this line.

00:48:35.156 --> 00:48:37.305
So, in the MNIST case,

00:48:37.306 --> 00:48:40.010
most of them
actually behave okay.

00:48:40.011 --> 00:48:43.465
And that's because this dataset
is not that complex.

00:48:43.466 --> 00:48:46.220
But once you actually
analyze other datasets,

00:48:46.221 --> 00:48:47.855
for example, five or 10,

00:48:47.856 --> 00:48:49.080
you will see that actually

00:48:49.081 --> 00:48:52.140
the center-based
approaches especially

00:48:52.141 --> 00:48:54.320
improve the accuracy on

00:48:54.321 --> 00:48:58.145
those classes that
are imbalanced.

00:48:58.146 --> 00:49:01.710
Well, you can see that
actually, the polling is hard.

00:49:01.711 --> 00:49:04.810
So, softmax tends to bias
a lot from classes in the head,

00:49:04.811 --> 00:49:06.970
meaning those that are
very well represented,

00:49:06.971 --> 00:49:08.630
but it does very poorly

00:49:08.631 --> 00:49:11.175
with classes that are not
very well represented.

00:49:11.176 --> 00:49:13.650
Now, when we increase
the scale of the datasets,

00:49:13.651 --> 00:49:16.250
so it'll be harder to note
improvement here though.

00:49:16.251 --> 00:49:17.885
But there is some improvement.

00:49:17.886 --> 00:49:21.530
So here, if you look closely,

00:49:21.531 --> 00:49:25.620
the black dots are
actually above the line

00:49:25.621 --> 00:49:28.430
which means that we're

00:49:28.431 --> 00:49:29.780
actually observing
an improvement

00:49:29.781 --> 00:49:31.585
thanks to the
center-based approach.

00:49:31.586 --> 00:49:33.600
And same here with
another dataset

00:49:33.601 --> 00:49:35.090
that is called Tiny ImageNet.

00:49:35.091 --> 00:49:37.210
So, this is the proposed
approach and you will

00:49:37.211 --> 00:49:39.610
see some dots that
are above the line.

00:49:39.611 --> 00:49:42.965
So, which means that this
approach is somewhat helping.

00:49:42.966 --> 00:49:48.630
And just to clearly emphasize
what's going on here,

00:49:48.631 --> 00:49:52.290
I summarized
the relative performance

00:49:52.291 --> 00:49:54.020
for classes in
the head and classes

00:49:54.021 --> 00:49:55.930
in the tail for
different methods.

00:49:55.931 --> 00:49:57.680
Here, I didn't consider
the Gaussian mix

00:49:57.681 --> 00:49:58.940
and model, because
actually as you saw,

00:49:58.941 --> 00:50:00.510
it is not performing well,

00:50:00.511 --> 00:50:03.060
and for all the datasets
that I consider.

00:50:03.061 --> 00:50:05.665
So, for the head,

00:50:05.666 --> 00:50:07.000
the proposed approach is

00:50:07.001 --> 00:50:09.080
actually showing
some drop in performance,

00:50:09.081 --> 00:50:10.460
but it's actually not that much.

00:50:10.461 --> 00:50:12.080
So, it's very close to Softmax.

00:50:12.081 --> 00:50:14.955
But when you look at
the classes in the tail,

00:50:14.956 --> 00:50:17.040
you can see that number

00:50:17.041 --> 00:50:18.590
is actually larger
than one which means

00:50:18.591 --> 00:50:20.465
that there is on
average some improvement

00:50:20.466 --> 00:50:22.630
in terms of classification.

00:50:22.631 --> 00:50:25.010
All right. So, this is one of

00:50:25.011 --> 00:50:27.380
the current projects that I
have I'm still working on.

00:50:27.381 --> 00:50:29.800
And then I also
want to talk about

00:50:29.801 --> 00:50:32.820
another project
that is visualizing

00:50:32.821 --> 00:50:34.455
a few things from

00:50:34.456 --> 00:50:38.850
CNNs that are used
for pixel-level tasks.

00:50:38.851 --> 00:50:42.835
This is also work
done with Chunhui Liu,

00:50:42.836 --> 00:50:45.320
Aayush Bansal, and Deva Ramanan.

00:50:45.321 --> 00:50:47.510
And let me give
you the motivation

00:50:47.511 --> 00:50:49.385
of why I wanted to do this.

00:50:49.386 --> 00:50:52.340
So, we humans actually can

00:50:52.341 --> 00:50:56.595
learn very easily from
even a single example.

00:50:56.596 --> 00:51:01.270
Right? But we also have
the ability to say,

00:51:01.271 --> 00:51:03.815
"Hey, that object I've
never seen before."

00:51:03.816 --> 00:51:05.420
Right? So, we have that ability

00:51:05.421 --> 00:51:06.550
of discovering new things,

00:51:06.551 --> 00:51:09.925
and then learn new things by
just experiencing the world.

00:51:09.926 --> 00:51:11.260
That's something
that I would like

00:51:11.261 --> 00:51:14.620
to enabling in computing.

00:51:14.621 --> 00:51:17.225
So, some idea that
I got was, well,

00:51:17.226 --> 00:51:18.690
maybe I can actually discover

00:51:18.691 --> 00:51:20.560
that new knowledge assuming that

00:51:20.561 --> 00:51:22.320
that new knowledge
somehow looks like

00:51:22.321 --> 00:51:25.470
an outlier of the
already known knowledge.

00:51:25.471 --> 00:51:28.090
And I already have theory as I

00:51:28.091 --> 00:51:31.415
showed earlier using
the feature matching problem.

00:51:31.416 --> 00:51:34.850
But the problem there
is that the theory that

00:51:34.851 --> 00:51:36.090
I showed earlier was using

00:51:36.091 --> 00:51:37.630
the nearest neighbor classifier,

00:51:37.631 --> 00:51:40.620
and then CNNs are
somewhat different.

00:51:40.621 --> 00:51:42.890
So, somehow I have to
establish this link

00:51:42.891 --> 00:51:46.010
of the nearest neighbor
classifier in CNNs.

00:51:46.011 --> 00:51:50.500
So, that's what motivated
to study this problem here.

00:51:50.501 --> 00:51:54.070
And so, we introduce
something that is called

00:51:54.071 --> 00:51:55.460
the Compositional
Nearest Neighbors

00:51:55.461 --> 00:51:57.470
approach to visualize
what's going on here.

00:51:57.471 --> 00:51:59.090
So, I assume everybody's
familiar with

00:51:59.091 --> 00:52:02.070
Pix2Pix and maybe
results of SegNet.

00:52:02.071 --> 00:52:03.540
So, on the first row,

00:52:03.541 --> 00:52:06.700
I'm showing label
to images where

00:52:06.701 --> 00:52:08.110
the ideas that you pass

00:52:08.111 --> 00:52:10.480
the labels of
a facade in this case,

00:52:10.481 --> 00:52:11.740
and then CNN will generate

00:52:11.741 --> 00:52:15.140
an image that agrees
with these input.

00:52:15.141 --> 00:52:17.750
The Compositional
Nearest Neighbors is

00:52:17.751 --> 00:52:20.110
actually something
that is very simple.

00:52:20.111 --> 00:52:23.130
So, if you grab your train CNN,

00:52:23.131 --> 00:52:25.140
and then you grab the last layer

00:52:25.141 --> 00:52:27.810
of your convolutional
neural net,

00:52:27.811 --> 00:52:30.950
and then you grab
a specific patch

00:52:30.951 --> 00:52:33.005
corresponding to one pixel

00:52:33.006 --> 00:52:35.060
and use that as
a feature descriptor,

00:52:35.061 --> 00:52:36.840
then you can actually search for

00:52:36.841 --> 00:52:39.710
the nearest training patches
in your training set,

00:52:39.711 --> 00:52:42.040
and then create this image
that looks a lot

00:52:42.041 --> 00:52:44.565
like what the CNN is
actually generating.

00:52:44.566 --> 00:52:46.520
Even though it's
noisy, that may be

00:52:46.521 --> 00:52:48.435
because of the parameters
that we used,

00:52:48.436 --> 00:52:52.625
but somehow, we are able to
replicate the results of CNN.

00:52:52.626 --> 00:52:54.720
So, this is using Pix2Pix,

00:52:54.721 --> 00:52:58.185
this is using another CNN
which is SegNet.

00:52:58.186 --> 00:52:59.980
And as you can see here,

00:52:59.981 --> 00:53:01.675
if you just compare these two,

00:53:01.676 --> 00:53:03.770
they overall tend to have

00:53:03.771 --> 00:53:05.150
similar behavior even though

00:53:05.151 --> 00:53:06.585
there are some artifacts there.

00:53:06.586 --> 00:53:12.660
Now, this column, it is
classical nearest neighbor,

00:53:12.661 --> 00:53:14.950
where you will represent
the entire image

00:53:14.951 --> 00:53:18.095
using some bottleneck feature
of your architecture,

00:53:18.096 --> 00:53:19.490
and then just retrieve the most

00:53:19.491 --> 00:53:22.590
similar image from
your training set.

00:53:22.591 --> 00:53:25.120
So, just to be clear, say that

00:53:25.121 --> 00:53:28.125
I want to synthesize
the value of this pixel,

00:53:28.126 --> 00:53:31.300
what I will do is
grab a patch from

00:53:31.301 --> 00:53:34.390
the last layer of the CNN,

00:53:34.391 --> 00:53:35.870
use it as a feature descriptor,

00:53:35.871 --> 00:53:37.400
and then search in
my training set.

00:53:37.401 --> 00:53:39.440
So, in this case, I'm
visualizing that I've found that

00:53:39.441 --> 00:53:41.715
maybe this is a window
that looks like this.

00:53:41.716 --> 00:53:43.040
And then what the compositional

00:53:43.041 --> 00:53:44.250
nearest neighbor is doing is

00:53:44.251 --> 00:53:46.840
just grabbing the information
from the training pair,

00:53:46.841 --> 00:53:49.400
grabbing the RGV value
here, and copying it here.

00:53:49.401 --> 00:53:52.200
Okay? What is nice
about this approach

00:53:52.201 --> 00:53:54.675
is that this allows us
to get correspondence.

00:53:54.676 --> 00:53:59.950
Meaning that, I know why
this value is maybe brown,

00:53:59.951 --> 00:54:01.580
and that is because
it's coming from

00:54:01.581 --> 00:54:02.950
these training set that is

00:54:02.951 --> 00:54:06.025
very similar to in
appearance at least.

00:54:06.026 --> 00:54:07.660
&gt;&gt; So, you do this
is for pixel on

00:54:07.661 --> 00:54:09.800
average with patches
with overline?

00:54:09.801 --> 00:54:11.250
&gt;&gt; Yes, not at this time,

00:54:11.251 --> 00:54:13.390
but that's one of the steps
that we're considering.

00:54:13.391 --> 00:54:15.540
So, right now, it was sort
of like a proof of concept

00:54:15.541 --> 00:54:17.965
of seeing if this is
actually working or not.

00:54:17.966 --> 00:54:21.160
And somebody is actually
replicating the results.

00:54:21.161 --> 00:54:23.695
For example, something that
caught our attention was,

00:54:23.696 --> 00:54:26.980
why is it that the CNN
is drawing this red,

00:54:26.981 --> 00:54:28.670
I don't know what that is,

00:54:28.671 --> 00:54:30.325
but this red region.

00:54:30.326 --> 00:54:31.980
Well, we find that
it is actually coming

00:54:31.981 --> 00:54:34.260
from this image that has that.

00:54:34.261 --> 00:54:36.190
So it's somehow is
actually replicating that.

00:54:36.191 --> 00:54:38.260
And so, we came to
the conclusion that maybe

00:54:38.261 --> 00:54:41.510
it's memorizing
the training set somehow,

00:54:41.511 --> 00:54:43.110
and then it does some sort of

00:54:43.111 --> 00:54:46.165
a fast and nearest
neighbor synthesis

00:54:46.166 --> 00:54:49.330
which is similar to what
we're proposing here.

00:54:49.331 --> 00:54:53.295
These are more results
on the same idea.

00:54:53.296 --> 00:54:56.245
Datasets are Facades,
CityScape, and CamVid.

00:54:56.246 --> 00:54:59.085
So, these are
results for Pix2Pix.

00:54:59.086 --> 00:55:00.740
These are for SegNet.

00:55:00.741 --> 00:55:02.575
And so, as you can see here,

00:55:02.576 --> 00:55:05.385
Pix2Pix and the Compositional
Neural Networks,

00:55:05.386 --> 00:55:10.030
they actually tend to look
very similar to each other.

00:55:10.031 --> 00:55:11.910
Same here for SegNet.

00:55:11.911 --> 00:55:15.650
And so, it looks
like the networks are

00:55:15.651 --> 00:55:17.480
memorizing somehow the dataset

00:55:17.481 --> 00:55:20.075
and then replicating
that information.

00:55:20.076 --> 00:55:24.145
And some interesting feature
as well is that,

00:55:24.146 --> 00:55:27.745
that patch that we're using
as a pixel representation,

00:55:27.746 --> 00:55:29.700
we know for sure that actually

00:55:29.701 --> 00:55:32.040
encodes sort of
global information,

00:55:32.041 --> 00:55:33.400
but also local information.

00:55:33.401 --> 00:55:34.740
By global information,

00:55:34.741 --> 00:55:37.225
I mean for example,
the color of the Facade.

00:55:37.226 --> 00:55:39.530
And for the local
information, for example,

00:55:39.531 --> 00:55:41.760
what kind of window style
are we rendering.

00:55:41.761 --> 00:55:43.690
So somehow, the network
is able to figure

00:55:43.691 --> 00:55:46.345
that out and embed
that information there.

00:55:46.346 --> 00:55:49.840
More results on other datasets

00:55:49.841 --> 00:55:53.030
from labels to images and then,

00:55:53.031 --> 00:55:57.220
from images to
Google Maps like images.

00:55:57.221 --> 00:55:58.700
So, as you can see here, we

00:55:58.701 --> 00:56:02.670
replicate a very,
very similar result.

00:56:02.671 --> 00:56:08.900
Now, another
interesting observation

00:56:08.901 --> 00:56:09.995
that we saw here is that

00:56:09.996 --> 00:56:13.000
what if instead of rendering

00:56:13.001 --> 00:56:15.960
the expected output
given by the pair, right?

00:56:15.961 --> 00:56:17.530
Because there's
image translation,

00:56:17.531 --> 00:56:19.745
can we reconstruct
the input image

00:56:19.746 --> 00:56:21.690
using the same approach?

00:56:21.691 --> 00:56:25.420
And it actually turned
out to be good as well.

00:56:25.421 --> 00:56:28.070
So, if we use
the same representation,

00:56:28.071 --> 00:56:31.410
but reconstruct the input
from the training set,

00:56:31.411 --> 00:56:33.960
we can actually do so.

00:56:33.961 --> 00:56:37.255
So, for example, consider
this row over here.

00:56:37.256 --> 00:56:41.075
We were able to
input this image,

00:56:41.076 --> 00:56:44.100
which has never seen
before by the CNN,

00:56:44.101 --> 00:56:46.550
and then by doing this
Compositional Nueral networks,

00:56:46.551 --> 00:56:47.875
we can actually reconstruct it.

00:56:47.876 --> 00:56:51.155
It actually resembles
the scene very well.

00:56:51.156 --> 00:56:53.630
So, this is something that got

00:56:53.631 --> 00:56:56.000
our attention and it

00:56:56.001 --> 00:56:58.500
looks like the
presentation is so good

00:56:58.501 --> 00:57:01.815
that it is even encoding
things from the input

00:57:01.816 --> 00:57:04.320
that is allowing us

00:57:04.321 --> 00:57:05.810
basically to just find

00:57:05.811 --> 00:57:07.550
the information from
the training set,

00:57:07.551 --> 00:57:09.280
and they just copy and paste in

00:57:09.281 --> 00:57:12.090
the information as
the final output.

00:57:12.091 --> 00:57:14.660
So, this happens
also with other type

00:57:14.661 --> 00:57:16.840
of data like labels.

00:57:16.841 --> 00:57:21.440
&gt;&gt; So, you don't have
any smoothness constraint

00:57:21.441 --> 00:57:22.920
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Yeah. Nothing.
Nothing like that.

00:57:22.921 --> 00:57:24.270
&gt;&gt; How do you make sure
the patches are coming

00:57:24.271 --> 00:57:26.115
for the same image?

00:57:26.116 --> 00:57:29.180
Why isn't there different
colours or different

00:57:29.181 --> 00:57:31.295
&gt;&gt; That's what I'm was
saying here before,

00:57:31.296 --> 00:57:33.470
that if somehow
the network learns that,

00:57:33.471 --> 00:57:35.740
so it contains
this global information

00:57:35.741 --> 00:57:38.980
exactly of what color were
the Facade we're rendering,

00:57:38.981 --> 00:57:41.315
what kind of style of Facade.

00:57:41.316 --> 00:57:43.700
But also local information,

00:57:43.701 --> 00:57:46.020
the windows information
for example,

00:57:46.021 --> 00:57:47.330
what kind of window is it?

00:57:47.331 --> 00:57:48.800
Is there a glass there?

00:57:48.801 --> 00:57:51.640
So, these are intriguing
results that we're

00:57:51.641 --> 00:57:57.595
still working on
this. But, yeah.

00:57:57.596 --> 00:58:01.180
Now, the last experiment.

00:58:01.181 --> 00:58:03.340
If you're familiar with Pix2Pix,

00:58:03.341 --> 00:58:05.130
they're using
a unit architecture.

00:58:05.131 --> 00:58:06.670
And it has this
Bottleneck and then,

00:58:06.671 --> 00:58:09.350
you have a lot of
the decoding layers.

00:58:09.351 --> 00:58:11.770
So, it looks like

00:58:11.771 --> 00:58:15.470
that the network is actually
using the Bottleneck to

00:58:15.471 --> 00:58:18.560
retrieve similar images and

00:58:18.561 --> 00:58:21.740
probably use the most
similar one as the basis.

00:58:21.741 --> 00:58:23.060
And then, as you progress

00:58:23.061 --> 00:58:25.315
over the deconvolutional layers,

00:58:25.316 --> 00:58:29.120
somewhat is morphing the output
by adding more details,

00:58:29.121 --> 00:58:30.750
and details, and
details until you reach

00:58:30.751 --> 00:58:32.670
to a final output.

00:58:32.671 --> 00:58:34.860
So, for example, this peak
that you observe here,

00:58:34.861 --> 00:58:36.980
which is the output of
the deconvolutional layer,

00:58:36.981 --> 00:58:38.510
it can be explained that it

00:58:38.511 --> 00:58:40.795
came from the most
similar image.

00:58:40.796 --> 00:58:46.050
Okay? So, it looks
like the CNN is just

00:58:46.051 --> 00:58:49.030
morphing for
every deconvolution that

00:58:49.031 --> 00:58:52.750
you're actually doing until
you reach the final output.

00:58:52.751 --> 00:58:56.310
So, we're still working on this.

00:58:56.311 --> 00:58:59.535
We have more experiments
in one of the next steps.

00:58:59.536 --> 00:59:02.450
&gt;&gt; So, I can understand
this deconvolution.

00:59:02.451 --> 00:59:04.755
But, Bottleneck, how do
you configure bottleneck?

00:59:04.756 --> 00:59:07.575
&gt;&gt; When you do the Forward pass,

00:59:07.576 --> 00:59:09.830
you will get deactivation
of the Bottleneck and use

00:59:09.831 --> 00:59:11.900
that as a whole descriptor
of the entire.

00:59:11.901 --> 00:59:12.620
&gt;&gt; To start it to process?

00:59:12.621 --> 00:59:14.520
&gt;&gt; Yes, exactly. It looks like

00:59:14.521 --> 00:59:16.440
it's grabbing the
most similar in.

00:59:16.441 --> 00:59:17.895
&gt;&gt; As we go through
the convolutions

00:59:17.896 --> 00:59:19.020
here, you're also grabbing.

00:59:19.021 --> 00:59:19.690
&gt;&gt; Patches.

00:59:19.691 --> 00:59:20.365
&gt;&gt; Smaller and smaller?

00:59:20.366 --> 00:59:25.190
&gt;&gt; Exactly, yes.
That's exactly right.

00:59:25.191 --> 00:59:28.210
All right. So, that is
actually pretty much it.

00:59:28.211 --> 00:59:29.920
So, just to give
you some conclusions

00:59:29.921 --> 00:59:31.745
of what I presented today.

00:59:31.746 --> 00:59:34.040
So, I hope I convince you

00:59:34.041 --> 00:59:36.580
that Extreme Value Theory
is actually fairly,

00:59:36.581 --> 00:59:40.805
well, it's a good theory
to model rare data.

00:59:40.806 --> 00:59:43.765
I showed that with
a feature matching.

00:59:43.766 --> 00:59:46.470
It allows us to predict
good correspondences

00:59:46.471 --> 00:59:49.020
and also speed up
robust estimation.

00:59:49.021 --> 00:59:51.635
In terms of long-tail datasets,

00:59:51.636 --> 00:59:54.840
it looks like center-based
approaches are, I mean,

00:59:54.841 --> 00:59:56.480
it's giving some insight that

00:59:56.481 --> 00:59:58.855
the we maybe up to
something there,

00:59:58.856 --> 01:00:01.630
but we still need to
work on that more.

01:00:01.631 --> 01:00:05.580
And then, these visualization
framework to understand

01:00:05.581 --> 01:00:10.060
pixel-level task from CNNs
are still intriguing, right?

01:00:10.061 --> 01:00:14.370
And so, now, with all these
unseen inputs to the network,

01:00:14.371 --> 01:00:16.880
we can explain how it
will behave at least

01:00:16.881 --> 01:00:19.620
using this framework that
I introduced. All right.

01:00:19.621 --> 01:00:22.750
So, that actually
concludes my talk.

01:00:22.751 --> 01:00:24.770
If you are more interested about

01:00:24.771 --> 01:00:27.920
some other projects
that I have in AI,

